From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 17:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: msm8998: reboot the firmware on runtime resume

On msm8998 the firmware comes back from a TZ-restored power collapse
wedged: TZ accepts the resume, the CTRL_INIT poll passes, and then the
ARM9 services nothing -- WRAPPER_CPU_STATUS shows it out of WFI, a
manually fired host-to-ARM9 soft interrupt (CPU_IC_SOFTINT) is ignored,
and replies to pre-collapse commands occasionally surface only after
yet another collapse cycle. Clock rates, the wrapper unlock, the
threshold handshake and every CCF-visible resource were each verified
in place mid-hang. The restore itself is what is broken.

So stop trusting it: when the firmware was up before the collapse, take
the same path the sys_error recovery takes and reboot it -- shutdown,
fresh queues, PAS re-auth, boot, SYS_INIT. Runtime suspend only happens
with no active codec work (the vdec/venc children pin the core through
their parent), so in the common case there is no firmware state worth
restoring anyway.

ponytail: this trades power-collapse resume latency (~100ms of PAS
auth) for correctness. The proper fix is whatever downstream's TZ
restore contract has that mainline's does not; take it to linux-media.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c
index 2863c5f0d499..7eec533c5d4b 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c
@@ -725,6 +725,41 @@ static __maybe_unused int venus_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 	if (venus_stop_before(6))
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * msm8998's TZ restore hands back a wedged firmware: resume is
+	 * accepted, the CTRL_INIT poll passes, and the ARM9 then ignores
+	 * even a manually fired CPU_IC_SOFTINT. When the firmware was up
+	 * before this collapse, reboot it the way sys_error recovery does
+	 * instead of trusting the restore. The fw is dead, so there is no
+	 * HFI goodbye to say -- drop the state and start over.
+	 */
+	if (IS_V3(core) && core->state == CORE_INIT) {
+		mutex_lock(&core->lock);
+		core->state = CORE_UNINIT;
+		venus_shutdown(core);
+		mutex_unlock(&core->lock);
+
+		hfi_reinit(core);
+
+		ret = venus_boot(core);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = hfi_core_resume(core, true);
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_shutdown;
+
+		ret = hfi_core_init(core);
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_shutdown;
+
+		return 0;
+
+err_shutdown:
+		venus_shutdown(core);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
 	return hfi_core_resume(core, false);
 }
 
